# RSPICE-VERILOG-STIMULUS 1
#
# One rising edge per vector, sampled after it. Reset is synchronous in every
# block here, so the first two vectors establish a known state through the clock
# rather than around it.
#
# `din` then walks a sequence of values that are distinct from each other and
# from zero, which is what makes the pipeline depths readable straight off the
# trace: after reset is released, `bl2` shows the current `din` on the very next
# observation while `nb2` still shows zero and does not show that same value
# until two observations later. If those two columns ever move together, the
# scheduler is applying non-blocking updates eagerly.
#
# `din` is held constant for the last four vectors so both pipelines drain to
# the same steady value. A case that ends mid-flight cannot distinguish "the
# depths are wrong" from "the trace stopped early".
#
# `swap_x`/`swap_y` alternate on every clock throughout, independent of `din`.

module nba_ordering

input clk 1
input rst 1
input din 4

output nb2 4
output bl2 4
output swap_x 4
output swap_y 4

clock clk 5
step 10
settle 8

# Synchronous reset, held two clocks.
vector 1 0000
vector 1 0000
# Four distinct values walk the pipelines.
vector 0 0001
vector 0 0010
vector 0 0100
vector 0 1000
# Hold, so both pipelines drain to the same steady value.
vector 0 1111
vector 0 1111
vector 0 1111
vector 0 1111
